  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 247 AM EDT Fri May 22 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis: \n\n\nA midlevel split flow pattern builds across North America this \nmorning with shortwave ridging currently overhead and upstream \ntroughing over the U.S. Subsidence aloft will result in one more day \nof relatively dry and quiet weather across the Great Lakes Region \nbefore precipitation chances build this weekend. The central U.S. \ntrough will lift across the Midwest, with weak low pressure tracking \nthrough the Ohio Valley. \n\n\nA second 500mb ridge begins to build over the American Southwest \nthis weekend and will progress across the CONUS early next week. \nThis pattern will support surface high pressure and overall quiet \nweather from Monday through midweek next week. Warmer Pacific air \ninfluence is expected to build across the Great Lakes Region next \nweek, causing surface temperatures to build warmer than normal \nthrough the remainder of the forecast period. \n\n\nForecast Details:\n\n\nToday...Surface high pressure remains settled directly over the \nGreat Lakes region, providing one final day of relatively pleasant \nweather. Cloud cover will continue to build from the south across \nthe CWA, with highs in the 60s for most areas. Only concern in terms \nof impactful weather will be the favorable fire weather conditions \nas dew point depressions increase in the afternoon. Critical RH \nvalues will likely be reached by midday for areas west of the I-75 \ncorridor as east flow gusts into the mid/upper 20s.  \n\n\nSaturday and Sunday... A weak troughing pattern with attached \nsurface low pressure will return rainfall Saturday afternoon. Latest \nCAM guidance depicts low pressure moving further to our southeast, \nfavoring light stratiform rainfall for areas closer to Saginaw Bay \nwhile the northern half of the CWA remains mostly dry. QPF totals of \na quarter inch or less are only expected for counties along the Lake \nHuron/Saginaw Bay Border  while the remainder of the CWA will likely \nonly observe a trace or so this Saturday. Low level moisture will \ncontinue isolated showers this Sunday, but again, most areas will \nlikely observe overcast skies with only a trace of additional \nprecipitation.  \n\n\nMonday through Thursday... The aforementioned ridging pattern builds \nover the Southwest U.S. this weekend and makes its way to the Great \nLakes Region by Memorial Day. Subsidence aloft will support the \ncontinuation of high pressure and overall quiet weather. Warm air \nadvection from a Pacific air influence over the Great Lakes Region \nwill cause surface temperatures to gradually build next week. Highs \nwill be in the mid to upper 70s this Monday and then reach the mid \n80s for most locations by the end of the forecast period.",
